    "result_text": "Terms now count business days, and all 4 tests in the spec pass when run with `bin/e2e`.\n\n**How it works now:** each claim keeps the terms it was given when it was created and the date they count from. Its due-by is no longer stored. Every time claims are read, the due-by is worked out as the terms-th business day after that date, skipping Saturdays, Sundays and whatever holidays are on the sheet at that moment. So adding a holiday moves the due-by of claims already on the sheet, not only new ones. Overdue, the late charge's boundaries, which period a claim belongs to, claim numbering and the closed-period checks all use that worked-out due-by, so they follow without other changes.\n\n**Changes:**\n- **Database:** a new migration, `V52__claim_business_day_terms.sql`, adds the terms and the counting-from date to `claim` and `claim_history`, and drops the old `due_by` column.\n- **Server:**\n  - `BusinessDays.java` is new and does the business-day count.\n  - `HolidayRepository` now gives the sheet's holiday dates and a due-by.\n  - `DueBy` now works out a new claim's terms and its due-by in business days.\n  - `ClaimRepository` stores the terms and works out each claim's due-by when it reads claims, including the as-of view.\n  - Adding a claim, splitting one and the quick-add closed-period check all use the new count. The old \"move a due-by off a holiday\" method is removed.\n- **Front end:** adding a holiday now re-reads the whole sheet, so due-bys, overdue figures and periods update straight away. I also updated the related comments and the visible wording (labels say \"business days\"; the holidays note explains the rule).\n\n**Decisions for you:**\n- **Changing a claim's date doesn't move its due-by.** Terms still count from the date the claim had when it was created; the spec's re-date and edit-date cases expect exactly this.\n- **Claims stored before this change are approximated.** The migration takes their terms to be the number of calendar days between their date and their old due-by. That can come out wrong if a holiday had pushed the due-by later or the claim was re-dated. Getting it exactly right would mean replaying the dated terms settings, which I didn't do. In practice the database is in-memory and starts empty on every run, so it only matters if real data is ever migrated.",
